  "meta": {
    "title": "#a − #b divisible by 3",
    "blurb": "A three-state cycle: a turns it one way, b the other. It accepts when the difference between the number of a’s and b’s is a multiple of 3 — a regular shadow of the non-regular “equal numbers of a and b”.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "ε"
      },
      {
        "w": "ab",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "aaa",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "bbb",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "aabbabba",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "a",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "aab",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "bbbb",
        "expect": "reject"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "modular-counting",
        "cyclic-group"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intro",
      "readme": "The states are the integers modulo 3, and the machine is the cyclic group ℤ/3 acting on them: **a** adds one, **b** subtracts one. A word is accepted when the net count lands back on 0.\n\nThe exact version — *equal* numbers of a's and b's — needs an unbounded counter, and no DFA recognises it: see **Equal a’s and b’s** among the counter automata. Working modulo 3 is what makes the memory finite."
    }
  }
}
